is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: switch_server_access
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: OSPF routing on the server access L3 switch (switch_server_access).

Mechanism: The user pods’ default gateways (10.1.1.1 and 10.2.1.1) return ICMP “Destination Net Unreachable” when probing 10.200.0.x, which indicates the distribution layer lacks a route to the data center subnet. Meanwhile, servers on 10.200.0.0/24 can reach each other and their gateway 10.200.0.1 because they are on the same L2/L3 segment locally.

Localization: On switch_server_access, vtysh reports “ospfd is not running,” and its FRR running config contains no router ospf stanza. Its routing table shows only connected routes (C>* 10.200.0.0/24 via br0 and C>* 172.16.0.10/31 via eth5). Router_core_3, which uplinks to switch_server_access on eth2 (172.16.0.10/31), has no OSPF neighbor on that interface, confirming no adjacency. Both distribution routers (switch_dist_1_1 and switch_dist_2_1) and cores have no route to 10.200.0.0/24, matching the net-unreachable seen from 10.1.1.1/10.2.1.1 during pings from hosts.

Root cause: OSPF on switch_server_access is disabled/misconfigured (ospfd not running and no OSPF configuration), so 10.200.0.0/24 is not advertised into the fabric.

Corrective fix: Restore OSPF on switch_server_access. Start FRR/ospfd and configure router ospf with the correct networks (e.g., 10.200.0.0/24 area X and 172.16.0.10/31 area X). Once the OSPF adjacency to router_core_3 forms, 10.200.0.0/24 will be installed on the distribution/core and user pods will regain reachability to DNS and the portals.
=== END ===